<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qaryat al-Faw</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as al-Faw</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qaryat al-Faw is an archaeological site in south-western Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, on the north-western edge of the Rub' al Khali (Empty Quarter). From about the 1st century BCE to the 5th century CE it was a caravan town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> route, and it is identified with Qaryat Dhat Kahl, an early capital of Kinda. Excavated by King Saud University from 1972, it has revealed markets, temples, houses, tombs and wall paintings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qaryat-al-faw.jpg" alt="Qaryat al-Faw" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pergamon-Museum_-_Bronzekopf.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies about 100 km south of Wadi ad-Dawasir and about 700 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a>, on the north-western border of the Empty Quarter, where the trade route linking the south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> with its north-east passed. The town had more than seventeen water <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, and its inhabitants also called it Qaryat al-Hamraa, the Red City, and Dhat al-Jnan, the City of Gardens. It was not fortified but was surrounded by extensive walls, and water reached it through planned canals and cisterns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its tutelary deity is thought to have been Kahl, the main god of the Arab tribes of Kindah and Madh'hij, and its pantheon also included the sun god Shams. Himyar exercised suzerainty over the region during the 1st to 3rd centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namara-inscription">Namara inscription</a> records an expedition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imru-al-qays-ibn-amr">Imru al-Qays ibn Amr</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a> in which he reached Qaryat al-Faw and drove the ruling tribe of Madh'hij from the city. The Kinda left it to settle in Hadhramaut, and the town was abandoned shortly afterwards.</p><p>The town was divided into quarters for the royal palace, temples and houses. Five temples of different periods have been found, among them the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadd">Wadd</a> near the palace. Kings and nobles were buried in stone-built chambers with towers above them, and a bust of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> came from this area. A temple of Shams held a mrzḥ, a ritual banqueting institution, the easternmost attested by inscriptions. Its frescoes of reclining banqueters, vine scrolls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> drivers and figures in Roman dress show Hellenistic influence that reached the town mainly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, with close parallels in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Fayum</a> mummy portraits. Juan de Lara dates the best-known fragment, inscribed zky, pious or blessed, probably to the 1st century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Workers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saudi-aramco">Saudi Aramco</a> oil company referred to the site in the 1940s, and three of its staff visited and wrote about it in 1952. Excavation by King Saud University uncovered a residential quarter of houses, squares, streets and a market, and a sacred quarter of temples and tombs. The Saudi government has fenced the site against looters. On 27 July 2024 the Cultural Landscape of Al-Faw Archaeological Area was inscribed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage List.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qaryat al-Faw” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Qaryat al-Faw</b>, a caravan town on the edge of the Empty Quarter, is often linked with the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kinda">Kingdom of Kinda</a></li></ul></section>
